readings.c2s2:
When ''this'' and ''that'' refer to foregoing words, ''this'', like the Latin ''hic'' or the French ''ceci'', refers to the last mentioned, the latter, and ''that'', like the Latin ''ille'' or the French ''cela'', to the first mentioned, the former.

{{smaller block|Self-love and reason to one end aspire,<br>Pain their aversion, pleasure their desire;<br>But greedy ''that'' its object would devour,<br>''This'' taste the honey and not wound the flower.
{{right|''Pope'', Essay on Man, ii. 89.}}}}

In all the above cases, ''that'', when referring to a plural noun, takes the plural form ''those'': as, ''that'' man, ''those'' men; give me ''that'', give me ''those''; and so on. (''c'') To represent a sentence or part of a sentence, or a series of sentences.

{{smaller block|And when Moses heard ''that'', he was content. Lev. x. 20.}}

{{smaller block|[''That'' here stands for the whole of what Aaron had said, or the whole of the preceding verse.]}}

{{smaller block|I'll know your business, Harry, ''that'' I will.
{{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. IV., ii. 3. 83.}}
Upon my conscience,<br>The man is truly honest, and ''that'' kills him.
{{right|''Fletcher'', Valentinian, iv. 3.}}
If the Laymen will not come, whose fault is ''that''?
{{right|''Selden'', Table-Talk, p. 37.}}
Certain or uncertain, be ''that'' upon the credit of those whom I must follow.
{{right|''Milton'', Hist. Eng., i.}}
They say he's learn'd as well as discreet, but I'm no judge of ''that''.
{{right|''Steele'', Lying Lover, i. 1.}}
You are a foolish bribble-brabble woman, ''that'' you are.
{{right|''Sir R. Howard'', The Committee, iii. 1.}}
Yet there still prevails, and ''that'' too amongst men who plume themselves on their liberality, no small amount of the feeling which Milton combated in his celebrated essay.
{{right|''H. Spencer'', Social Statics, p. 167.}}}}

''That'' sometimes in this use precedes the sentence or clause to which it refers.

{{smaller block|''That'' be far from thee, to do after this manner, ''to slay the righteous with the wicked.''
{{right|Gen. xviii. 25.}}}}

''That'' here represents the clause in italics. It is used also

