{{smaller block|[''That'' came in during the twelfth century to supply the place of the indeclinable relative ''the'', and in the fourteenth century it is the ordinary relative. In the sixteenth century, ''which'' often supplies its place; in the seventeenth century, ''who'' replaces it. About Addison's time, ''that'' had again come into fashion, and had almost driven ''which'' and ''who'' out of use.
{{right|''Morris'', Historical Outlines of Eng. Accidence, p. 132.]}}}}

